{"agent":"bfix2-lane4","data":{"by":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":2,"detail":"The change puts ACP's shared stdio plumbing inside the MCP adapter: `_ChannelClient`'s docstring says it is shared by the MCP `StdioClient` and the ACP client, and the new `send_line`/`finish_answer` helpers live in `mcp.py`. This violates [r-adapters]: an ACP implementation should live in the ACP adapter's own file, not depend on MCP-adapter internals. Concrete consequence: a later MCP-only edit to `send_line` or `_ChannelClient.close` (for example, changing which exceptions are swallowed or how `_channel` is cleared) silently changes `AcpStdioClient` behavior without any edit to `ddflow/adapters/acp.py` or its fake, so MCP changes can break ACP and parallel agents editing the two ports collide on `mcp.py`.","digest":"56d43b619e053d26","location":"ddflow/adapters/mcp.py:401 (_ChannelClient)","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/adapters/mcp.py:401 (_ChannelClient)"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"The new `_ChannelClient` is defined in the MCP adapter file and declares itself shared by “mcp `StdioClient`” and “the ACP client,” so the ACP port change reaches into `ddflow/adapters/mcp.py` instead of touching only its own files. This violates [r-adapters] (a new port touches only its own files) and the rule’s rationale that a change or swap should touch one place.","digest":"77b19e5314b5cd05","location":"ddflow/adapters/mcp.py:399 _ChannelClient","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/adapters/mcp.py:399 _ChannelClient"}],"chunks_total":4,"coverage":"4/4 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":13680,"diff_sha":"32515694bdf028541bd130e2c8e92139dd14930bddc655c94b81faccb5eb6d09","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":223.5,"family":"alibaba","findings":2,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8","output_digest":"f87cfabf1cb5e846","out