{"agent":"bfix2-lane4","data":{"by":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":2,"detail":"The new `_ChannelClient` is placed in the MCP adapter and explicitly documented as shared with the ACP client. That makes the ACP port depend on MCP adapter internals: the new `AcpStdioClient` in `ddflow/adapters/acp.py` cannot be imported or changed without pulling in `ddflow.adapters.mcp._ChannelClient`. Concretely, importing `ddflow.adapters.acp` after this change loads the MCP adapter file for the ACP client’s `close`/`_send` plumbing, so a breakage or edit in the MCP adapter can break ACP even though `mcp` and `acp` are separate external dependencies. This violates [r-adapters], which requires each port/adapter to touch only its own files so swaps and parallel changes stay isolated.","digest":"5ae3cd5b6e2d828e","location":"ddflow/adapters/mcp.py:_ChannelClient","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/adapters/mcp.py:_ChannelClient"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"This change adds the new ACP client’s shared plumbing (`_ChannelClient`, documented as “shared by … the ACP client”) in the MCP port file `ddflow/adapters/mcp.py`, while the manifest says the ACP port lives in `ddflow/adapters/acp.py`. That violates [r-adapters]: a new port should touch only its own files. The ACP adapter is now coupled to a private symbol in the MCP adapter file, so a change or fake for MCP can silently affect ACP and parallel agents can collide on `mcp.py`.","digest":"1499e8d5d212e9fc","location":"ddflow/adapters/mcp.py: _ChannelClient","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/adapters/mcp.py: _ChannelClient"}],"chunks_total":4,"coverage":"4/4 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":13680,"diff_sha":"32515694bdf028541bd130e2c8e92139dd14930bddc655c94b81faccb5eb6d09","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":214.4,"family":"alibaba","findings":2,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model